00:35:20,668 --> 00:35:21,889
that's the Cambrian period.

620
00:35:22,189 --> 00:35:26,291
Following the Cambrian, the Ordovician period, the Silurian, the Devonian, the Carboniferous,

621
00:35:26,331 --> 00:35:28,592
the Permian. Over the course of this time,

622
00:35:28,632 --> 00:35:31,854
we see the rise of all the different types of animals that we have today,

623
00:35:31,874 --> 00:35:34,255
the major groups of animals and plants that we have today.

624
00:35:34,644 --> 00:35:38,026
Things diversify in the ocean, the Devonian is the age of fishes,

625
00:35:38,606 --> 00:35:42,408
then plants and animals and then vertebrate animals make it onto land,

626
00:35:42,468 --> 00:35:47,030
and we see the rise of forests and the rise of amphibians and the rise of reptiles.

627
00:35:47,610 --> 00:35:49,891
All of this diversification, all of this wonderful history,

628
00:35:50,152 --> 00:35:53,953
ends at the end of the Permian with the Permian mass extinction,

629
00:35:54,344 --> 00:35:59,287
which leads the way into the Mesozoic era, which is called the Age of Reptiles.

630
00:35:59,767 --> 00:36:05,109
The Mesozoic is split into three periods of its own, the Triassic, the Jurassic, and the Cretaceous,

631
00:36:05,590 --> 00:36:11,192
and those are notable because that's the time periods where we find dinosaurs and pterosaurs

632
00:36:11,512 --> 00:36:16,015
and giant marine reptiles like mosasaurs and plesiosaurs and things like that.

633
00:36:16,315 --> 00:36:18,496
We see the rise of modern amphibians.

634
00:36:18,985 --> 00:36:23,447
In this time period, we see the rise of turtles and crocodilians and mammals and birds.

635
00:36:23,467 --> 00:36:25,268
-All the good stuff. -All the good stuff.

636
00:36:25,728 --> 00:36:29,110
And then the Mesozoic ends at the end of the Cretaceous period

637
00:36:29,590 --> 00:36:34,232
with the KPG mass extinction that we talked about back in episode 5.

