and look at the walls of the canyon, you will see all these layers of rock.

710
00:41:00,467 --> 00:41:01,727
Have these bands of earth.

711
00:41:02,008 --> 00:41:06,409
Yeah, every layer is a record of a past environment.

712
00:41:06,750 --> 00:41:10,131
The sediments that make it up, the features that are present there,

713
00:41:10,151 --> 00:41:16,394
the fossils that you find there are clues to an ecosystem, an environment, a habitat that used to be.

714
00:41:16,806 --> 00:41:23,269
If you find, right, limestone is formed in shallow oceans, right, where you get things like coral reefs.

715
00:41:23,769 --> 00:41:28,331
Sandstone might come from a beach, it might come from a riverbed, it might come from a desert,

716
00:41:28,731 --> 00:41:33,253
and depending on where it was formed, you will see different features in the sand,

717
00:41:33,634 --> 00:41:35,314
and you will see different kinds of fossils.

718
00:41:35,895 --> 00:41:39,636
So one of the jobs of a geologist is to look at a layer of rock and say,

719
00:41:39,664 --> 00:41:42,525
all right, this is what this rock was, right?

720
00:41:42,565 --> 00:41:45,527
This was a forest, this was a swamp.

721
00:41:45,707 --> 00:41:49,149
Yeah, figure out what it took to form that formation.

722
00:41:49,489 --> 00:41:53,871
Yes, and then when you look at your Grand Canyon, for example,

723
00:41:53,931 --> 00:41:56,652
again, if you're looking at many, many layers on top of each other,

724
00:41:57,033 --> 00:41:59,274
what you're seeing is changing environments.

725
00:42:00,084 --> 00:42:06,448
Your beach becomes a reef, your reef becomes a beach again as sea level rises and falls.

726
00:42:06,848 --> 00:42:10,170
Your swamp becomes a desert as the climate changes, right?

727
00:42:10,190 --> 00:42:14,112
You're looking at changing environments over time.

728
00:42:15,092 --> 00:42:21,376
A succession of past environments and conditions over the course of Earth's history.

729
00:42:21,586 --> 00:42:26,188
Yeah, and as they change environments, this changes the rock that they lay down.

730
00:42:26,668 --> 00:42:26,928
Yup.

731
00:42:27,508 --> 00:42:31,510
And it will change what lives there, which changes the kinds of fossils that you're going to find.

732
00:42:32,050 --> 00:42:37,412
And so this is sort of the basic foundation of geology is looking at these layers.

733
00:42:37,452 --> 00:42:40,674
What do they tell us and what do they tell us about the passage of time?

734
00:42:40,934 --> 00:42:41,134
Yes.

735
00:42:41,734 --> 00:42:46,296
In the 1600s is really when geology kind of started kicking off

736
00:42:47,064 --> 00:42:49,505
in the sense of investigating things like this.

737
00:42:50,025 --> 00:42:53,647
And a famous name that you will hear coming from this time period

738
00:42:54,168 --> 00:43:02,271
is Nicholas Steno. And Steno is famous for doing a thing that a lot of scientists get famous for,

739
00:43:02,692 --> 00:43:05,493
which is writing down obvious statements.

740
00:43:06,474 --> 00:43:06,734
Yes.

741
00:43:07,544 --> 00:43:12,286
Steno developed a lot of our modern principles of stratigraphy.

742
00:43:12,306 --> 00:43:17,229
Stratigraphy is the study of rock layers and the succession of rock layers.

743
00:43:17,849 --> 00:43:24,212
Steno wrote down such incredible insights as the law of superposition, which says

744
00:43:24,412 --> 00:43:26,193
the rock layers on the bottom are the oldest.

745
00:43:26,413 --> 00:43:26,553
Yup.

746
00:43:27,082 --> 00:43:33,786
Which is kind of duh, but it's not a well-known principle until somebody points it out.

747
00:43:34,066 --> 00:43:37,707
Yeah, that's something that it's always a good thing to remember,

748
00:43:37,868 --> 00:43:41,149
is that common sense things at some point were insightful.

749
00:43:41,349 --> 00:43:41,869
Yes, exactly.

750
00:43:42,810 --> 00:43:46,051
But it makes perfect sense that, you know, you can't build a house from the top.

751
00:43:46,892 --> 00:43:48,373
The bottom layer is the oldest.

752
00:43:48,944 --> 00:43:53,767
He pointed out things like the fact that rock layers are laid down horizontally,

753
00:43:54,607 --> 00:44:01,751
that rock layers in different parts of your region should be contiguous and contemporaneous.

754
00:44:01,811 --> 00:44:04,072
That is to say that they correlate to each other, right?

755
00:44:04,092 --> 00:44:06,013
It's the same time.

756
00:44:06,373 --> 00:44:06,553
Yeah.

757
00:44:06,593 --> 00:44:08,934
It's the same, even if it's not the same environment, right?

758
00:44:09,163 --> 00:44:14,346
You might have rocks that represent a beach, and if you follow them west far enough,

759
00:44:14,787 --> 00:44:17,968
they grade into rocks that represent the ocean.

760
00:44:18,749 --> 00:44:21,931
Because the whole world wasn't a beach, you had different environments in different places.

761
00:44:22,591 --> 00:44:25,273
So you're putting all of this together and you're basically

762
00:44:25,453 --> 00:44:31,777
looking at what changes am I seeing, how can I infer the changes over time.

763
00:44:31,997 --> 00:44:32,197
Yeah.

764
00:44:32,464 --> 00:44:34,965
But not every place is like the Grand Canyon.

765
00:44:35,445 --> 00:44:35,906
No.

766
00:44:36,306 --> 00:44:36,766
Unfortunately.

767
00:44:36,926 --> 00:44:37,166
Yeah.

768
00:44:37,546 --> 00:44:42,468
And even the Grand Canyon only has a chunk of Earth history.

769
00:44:43,349 --> 00:44:48,011
So one of the big jobs of geologists, especially early on in geologic study,

770
00:44:48,411 --> 00:44:51,973
was correlating layers across the world.

771
00:44:52,133 --> 00:44:52,393
Yeah.

772
00:44:52,693 --> 00:44:55,534
It's a big cooperative effort.

773
00:44:55,774 --> 00:44:56,174
Indeed.

774
00:44:56,583 --> 00:45:00,305
And basically what you're doing there is you're looking for, all right, I have a beach here.

775
00:45:00,906 --> 00:45:06,489
What global effects can I find to match between different layers in different parts of the world?

776
00:45:06,849 --> 00:45:09,791
So to use an extraordinarily famous example, which we talked about,

777
00:45:09,811 --> 00:45:16,274
we devoted an entire episode to, the KPG boundary is the layer of clay

778
00:45:16,685 --> 00:45:21,648
below which you find ammonites and big forams and dinosaurs and pterosaurs,

779
00:45:22,088 --> 00:45:24,509
and above which you don't find any of those things.

780
00:45:25,349 --> 00:45:28,611
You can find this same layer in many places in the world.

781
00:45:28,631 --> 00:45:31,912
You see it in Colorado, you can see it in Antarctica.

782
00:45:31,932 --> 00:45:33,433
I don't know if you can see it in Australia.

783
00:45:33,733 --> 00:45:34,814
That I do not know.

784
00:45:35,074 --> 00:45:35,454
Look it up.

785
00:45:35,574 --> 00:45:36,374
We'll have to look it up later.

786
00:45:36,494 --> 00:45:36,914
Yes, we will.

787
00:45:37,195 --> 00:45:40,936
But basically you say, all right, well, I have this sequence of layers,

788
00:45:41,104 --> 00:45:43,925
which represents a particular event, right?

789
00:45:43,945 --> 00:45:47,107
We're looking at an event represented by the fossils, right?

790
00:45:47,147 --> 00:45:50,509
That life changed, represented by this clay layer,

791
00:45:50,549 --> 00:45:52,710
which is conveniently laid down all over the planet.

792
00:45:52,950 --> 00:45:53,190
Yep.

793
00:45:53,390 --> 00:45:56,792
And if you find it at the top of your layers over here in Colorado

794
00:45:56,852 --> 00:45:59,233
and the bottom of your layers over there in Alberta,

795
00:45:59,654 --> 00:46:05,417
now you can stack those on top of each other and you are building this timeline.

796
00:46:05,846 --> 00:46:08,907
It acts as a baseline, a common reference point.

797
00:46:09,127 --> 00:46:09,408
Yes.

798
00:46:10,068 --> 00:46:12,289
So you can look at geological events, right?

799
00:46:12,309 --> 00:46:15,090
You can look at the changing sea levels.

800
00:46:15,331 --> 00:46:17,992
You can look at changing positions of the continents.

801
00:46:18,552 --> 00:46:24,275
Probably the biggest thing that is used for correlation of layers across time,

802
00:46:24,315 --> 00:46:28,437
especially early on, was faunal succession, right?

803
00:46:28,697 --> 00:46:29,978
What fossils do you see?

804
00:46:30,044 --> 00:46:31,044
What was life like?

805
00:46:31,164 --> 00:46:31,504
Yeah.

806
00:46:31,744 --> 00:46:33,865
Because like in that previous example,

807
00:46:33,905 --> 00:46:36,026
there were dinosaurs and then there weren't dinosaurs.

808
00:46:36,247 --> 00:46:39,548
And then if you've got mammals in your timeline,

809
00:46:39,628 --> 00:46:43,630
then that helps you figure out where it fits along with these other layers.

810
00:46:43,990 --> 00:46:47,332
Yeah, you can at least know it was before or after certain events.

811
00:46:47,792 --> 00:46:48,232
Indeed.

812
00:46:48,752 --> 00:46:52,794
So you put all this together and geologists work all over the globe to do this.

813
00:46:53,304 --> 00:46:57,926
And eventually what you have is a sequence of rock layers

814
00:46:57,986 --> 00:47:03,168
that you have correlated across the planet that start all the way at the beginning

815
00:47:03,609 --> 00:47:05,449
and go all the way to the present.

816
00:47:05,629 --> 00:47:05,790
Yep.

817
00:47:06,070 --> 00:47:11,292
That same timeline that we just ran through real quick at the beginning of this episode

818
00:47:11,712 --> 00:47:13,233
is represented in those rocks.

819
00:47:13,873 --> 00:47:14,394
Exactly.

820
00:47:14,414 --> 00:47:17,935
And so I've heard lots of people compare them to pages of a book,

821
00:47:18,583 --> 00:47:21,044
which is always a nice way to visualize it,

822
00:47:21,104 --> 00:47:25,747
is it's a book laying on its side with chapter one down against the table.

823
00:47:26,047 --> 00:47:27,047
Yes, exactly.

824
00:47:27,647 --> 00:47:33,350
One of the nice things about this is that it's putting together your history of the planet

825
00:47:33,390 --> 00:47:34,471
and your sequence of events.

826
00:47:35,051 --> 00:47:37,892
One of the really nice things and one of the really encouraging things

827
00:47:38,533 --> 00:47:42,755
is that your sequence of events is always the same.

828
00:47:43,685 --> 00:47:49,468
So when you're putting all this together, it's tempting to ask, and you should ask,

829
00:47:49,928 --> 00:47:52,389
how do we know that we're getting this right?

830
00:47:52,429 --> 00:47:53,990
How do we know we're getting the order right?

831
00:47:54,010 --> 00:47:56,531
How do we know that we're not missing pieces?

832
00:47:57,292 --> 00:47:59,913
And the answer is, well, first of all, you are missing pieces.

833
00:47:59,953 --> 00:48:02,114
That's why you're correlating across different parts of the world.

834
00:48:02,314 --> 00:48:02,494
Yep.

835
00:48:02,674 --> 00:48:07,356
But the wonderful thing is that there are so many sequences of stratigraphy around the world,

836
00:48:07,364 --> 00:48:09,284
there are so many preserved rock layers,

837
00:48:09,605 --> 00:48:12,846
that you get to come at this from many, many different angles.

838
00:48:13,727 --> 00:48:15,528
And this allows you to look for a word

839
00:48:15,568 --> 00:48:18,389
that I am going to bring up over and over again over the course of this episode,

840
00:48:18,729 --> 00:48:20,370
and that word is corroboration.

841
00:48:20,610 --> 00:48:20,850
Yes.

842
00:48:21,270 --> 00:48:23,992
The same, different layers in different parts of the world

843
00:48:24,052 --> 00:48:26,453
are going to show you the same sequence of events.

844
00:48:27,073 --> 00:48:31,715
When you find that KPG layer, it will always be on top of dinosaurs and below mammals.

845
00:48:32,845 --> 00:48:37,947
When you find trilobites, you will not ever find trilobites and dinosaurs in the same place.

846
00:48:38,308 --> 00:48:39,948
Yeah, exactly.

847
00:48:39,968 --> 00:48:43,510
You will never find humans in the same place as Indricatheres,

848
00:48:43,810 --> 00:48:48,612
or as stereospondyls, I guess, except for Sicilians if they're stereospondyls.

849
00:48:49,893 --> 00:48:52,054
Although you won't find them aside Sicilians anyway,

850
00:48:52,074 --> 00:48:53,735
because there's no Sicilians in the fossil record.

851
00:48:54,335 --> 00:48:57,516
You are always going to see this same sequence of events.

852
00:48:57,884 --> 00:48:58,084
Yeah.

853
00:48:58,264 --> 00:49:03,006
Trilobites down here, Pangaea happens here, dinosaurs go extinct here,

854
00:49:03,446 --> 00:49:05,527
humans show up here in the Ice Age up here.

855
00:49:05,827 --> 00:49:08,748
You're getting that reinforced over and over and over again,

856
00:49:09,189 --> 00:49:11,830
and that's where we put together this time scale.

857
00:49:12,150 --> 00:49:17,572
Yeah, that's the core idea behind science of corroboration

858
00:49:17,672 --> 00:49:22,054
is you see the same thing every time a subject is looked at,

859
00:49:22,566 --> 00:49:26,228
regardless of who's looking at it or how they're looking at it.

860
00:49:26,668 --> 00:49:33,211
They always find that gravity works and that these different things hold true.

861
00:49:33,271 --> 00:49:37,053
And this one's important because you all are only getting a piece of the puzzle.

862
00:49:37,173 --> 00:49:40,674
This is also why we only find certain fossils in certain parts of the country.

863
00:49:40,734 --> 00:49:46,297
Like people here in Florida always ask, "Oh, what kind of dinosaurs do we find here?"

864
00:49:46,657 --> 00:49:47,057
Not many.

865
00:49:47,324 --> 00:49:49,785
No, no, we don't, because you don't have the right rocks.

866
00:49:49,805 --> 00:49:51,126
Because we don't have the right rocks.

867
00:49:51,166 --> 00:49:54,948
We get a lot of really recent stuff, because once again that preserves much easily,

868
00:49:54,968 --> 00:49:58,330
and we have a lot of really old stuff, because that's what most of Florida is made out of.

869
00:49:58,570 --> 00:50:00,010
Very old rock.

870
00:50:00,270 --> 00:50:03,792
And so we get lots of way pre-dinosaur stuff,

871
00:50:04,192 --> 00:50:07,314
and lots of very young stuff after the dinosaurs.

872
00:50:07,694 --> 00:50:11,316
But because of this, the layers are not going to be all complete,

873
00:50:11,384 --> 00:50:14,645
or if a mountain rose up somewhere, it might have disrupted a layer.

874
00:50:14,925 --> 00:50:18,047
Yes, and indeed that's why if you go out to the Midwest,

875
00:50:18,087 --> 00:50:23,549
you get a lot of Mesozoic rocks, things like Cretaceous, things like that.

876
00:50:23,889 --> 00:50:30,652
But here in the east, where the Appalachians have shoved up all these rock layers from deep down,

877
00:50:30,672 --> 00:50:32,173
you get a lot of much older stuff.

878
00:50:32,433 --> 00:50:35,674
Yep, and so that's why the corroboration is so important,

879
00:50:35,864 --> 00:50:43,148
is you have to be comparing from where you can see the right layers to actually do the comparison.

880
00:50:43,468 --> 00:50:47,710
Yes, that's a really good point, that the right rocks only crop out in certain places,

881
00:50:48,270 --> 00:50:52,953
and that informs where scientists go to look for things.

882
00:50:52,973 --> 00:50:54,414
Yeah, that's a cool part.

883
00:50:54,654 --> 00:50:58,936
I was talking with Michelle Stocker at a conference recently who studies...

884
00:50:59,444 --> 00:51:01,845
She was studying phytosaurs, and that's why I was talking to her about those.

885
00:51:02,326 --> 00:51:04,807
If you don't know what a phytosaur is, we'll do a whole episode on phytosaurs.

886
00:51:04,947 --> 00:51:05,707
They're awesome.

887
00:51:05,867 --> 00:51:07,908
They're pretty cool. They're not crocs, but they're really trying to be.

888
00:51:08,168 --> 00:51:10,149
Yeah, they're almost there.

889
00:51:10,469 --> 00:51:13,551
And so what she's interested in is the middle of the Triassic.

890
00:51:14,131 --> 00:51:16,953
And the trouble with the middle of the Triassic is there's only so many places in the world

891
00:51:16,993 --> 00:51:19,334
you can go to find rocks of that age.

892
00:51:19,854 --> 00:51:23,716
And so she... I was talking to her about research that she...

893
00:51:24,004 --> 00:51:26,666
She's been working with others in places like Arizona.

894
00:51:27,446 --> 00:51:30,047
She was studying a fossil from China,

895
00:51:30,447 --> 00:51:34,909
and she was like a week away from heading off to field work in Tanzania,

896
00:51:35,089 --> 00:51:40,012
because that's where she needed to go to find the time period that she was looking for.

897
00:51:40,172 --> 00:51:40,472
Yeah.

898
00:51:40,672 --> 00:51:45,694
So it really does... It really is important to know this sequence of events to figure out

899
00:51:45,764 --> 00:51:50,266
where you need to go to find the part of history that you want to investigate.

900
00:51:50,306 --> 00:51:55,649
Yeah, it's... That's one of my favorite aspects of it is that you can aim for different areas.

901
00:51:55,749 --> 00:52:02,093
It's like, I want to find this aged fossils, so I know of five places that I could...

902
00:52:02,413 --> 00:52:04,814
I should go to most likely find some of that stuff.

903
00:52:04,874 --> 00:52:09,016
And it's, you know, some major discoveries have been found using that technique.

904
00:52:09,236 --> 00:52:09,617
Indeed.

905
00:52:09,843 --> 00:52:11,484
So we have this sequence of events.

906
00:52:12,064 --> 00:52:15,106
The next step is naming them all.

907
00:52:15,146 --> 00:52:19,688
So you've put all these rock layers together, but they're just rock layers, right?

908
00:52:19,868 --> 00:52:23,549
And the rock layers are different in different places.

909
00:52:24,070 --> 00:52:28,512
So this layer over here might just be a volcanic eruption,

910
00:52:28,532 --> 00:52:33,054
whereas this layer over here might be centuries of accumulation in a lake.

911
00:52:33,966 --> 00:52:40,009
So what we do is we look at all of this change and all of this diversity

912
00:52:40,089 --> 00:52:44,371
and the sequence of fossils and the sequence of continental movements and all that stuff,

913
00:52:44,871 --> 00:52:47,673
and we need to categorize things because that's what humans do.

914
00:52:47,813 --> 00:52:48,913
Yep, it's how our brains work.

915
00:52:49,134 --> 00:52:53,656
Just like in the Tree of Life episode, we're breaking the spectrum of life

916
00:52:54,036 --> 00:52:57,618
into discrete groups that we give names so that we can talk about them.

917
00:52:57,725 --> 00:52:58,685
And bite-sized bits.

918
00:52:59,045 --> 00:52:59,746
Bite-sized bits.

919
00:53:00,106 --> 00:53:01,506
We do the same thing with the geologic time.

920
00:53:02,267 --> 00:53:06,709
So all those names, the Hadean, Eon, the Cretaceous period, the Pleistocene, Epoch,

921
00:53:06,909 --> 00:53:08,109
those are all things we made up.

922
00:53:08,670 --> 00:53:15,212
Very much like the Tree of Life and the groupings that we identify there,

923
00:53:15,673 --> 00:53:18,654
those periods are based on something.

924
00:53:19,165 --> 00:53:25,868
And usually they represent time periods that you can recognize based on their features.

925
00:53:26,248 --> 00:53:30,270
Same way that an amphibian is a type of animal you recognize based on its features.

926
00:53:30,470 --> 00:53:37,173
Yes, there's a list of qualifiers that allowed us to denote a name to a sequence of time.

927
00:53:37,385 --> 00:53:44,108
Yes, so the boundaries between the layers typically represent major shifts.

928
00:53:45,088 --> 00:53:51,111
It is not an accident that the Cretaceous period ends with the K-P-G boundary

929
00:53:51,291 --> 00:53:52,791
and the K-P-G extinction.

930
00:53:53,112 --> 00:53:54,312
I always thought that was too convenient.

931
00:53:54,512 --> 00:53:54,812
Indeed.

932
00:53:55,152 --> 00:53:56,513
We named it that way, right?

933
00:53:56,593 --> 00:53:59,814
We called it, we said that's where it ends, right?

934
00:53:59,823 --> 00:54:04,866
The Triassic, the Jurassic, and the Cretaceous all end with mass extinction events.

935
00:54:05,166 --> 00:54:12,470
Because those extinction events rewrite the global ecosystems enough that geologists said,

936
00:54:12,490 --> 00:54:14,331
"All right, well, we better call this something different."

937
00:54:14,651 --> 00:54:18,673
Yeah, when they're also something that we can point to, that we can see.

938
00:54:18,913 --> 00:54:23,175
You know, we don't want to base a time scale on something that's very subtle and minute,

939
00:54:23,325 --> 00:54:26,907
because you may not be able to pick it out, so it's not worth.

940
00:54:27,347 --> 00:54:30,989
But if you can notice the change every time, then we can all say,

941
00:54:31,009 --> 00:54:34,431
"Oh, wait, we see the change. Now we know what rock we're dealing with."

942
00:54:34,751 --> 00:54:35,011
Indeed.

943
00:54:35,391 --> 00:54:39,233
It's very much like, you know, the archaeologists have a very similar...

944
00:54:39,573 --> 00:54:39,974
Yes.

945
00:54:40,034 --> 00:54:41,895
It's doing the same thing for human history.

946
00:54:42,275 --> 00:54:45,717
And you have the Stone Age, the Bronze Age, the Iron Age.

947
00:54:45,897 --> 00:54:46,117
Yep.

948
00:54:46,223 --> 00:54:49,065
And they're named for what was happening at that time.

949
00:54:49,165 --> 00:54:50,806
If you find a certain kind of artifact.

950
00:54:50,826 --> 00:54:53,507
If you find stone tools, there you go.

951
00:54:53,527 --> 00:54:57,389
If you find dinosaurs, you're in the Age of Dinosaurs.

952
00:54:57,669 --> 00:54:57,809
Yep.

953
00:54:58,009 --> 00:55:00,150
A lot of boundaries are based on extinction events.

954
00:55:00,571 --> 00:55:03,832
Some of them are based on dramatic changes in climate, right?

955
00:55:03,852 --> 00:55:07,094
The Pleistocene epoch is pretty much just the Ice Age.

956
00:55:07,414 --> 00:55:07,594
Yeah.

957
00:55:07,903 --> 00:55:13,046
Starts with the onset of glaciation, ends with the, quote, "end of glaciation,"

958
00:55:13,106 --> 00:55:16,148
although it's only been 11,000 years, so, you know.

959
00:55:16,708 --> 00:55:17,789
I'm sure we're fine.

960
00:55:18,729 --> 00:55:21,671
I'm sure there's some sort of reason why the glaciers won't come back.

961
00:55:21,791 --> 00:55:23,452
Yeah, but we're making sure.

962
00:55:25,053 --> 00:55:26,894
We're stacking the deck with it.

963
00:55:26,934 --> 00:55:27,994
That name is gonna stick.

964
00:55:29,864 --> 00:55:33,966
So these periods of time are, you know, eons, epochs, periods, eras,

965
00:55:34,146 --> 00:55:36,647
are times that we've divided time into.

966
00:55:37,068 --> 00:55:40,850
One of the interesting effects that happens is what we try to do,

967
00:55:40,990 --> 00:55:44,392
and for the most part we do, is find global patterns.

968
00:55:44,932 --> 00:55:45,152
Yes.

969
00:55:45,573 --> 00:55:48,774
Right, the dinosaurs didn't go extinct in one place, they went extinct everywhere.

970
00:55:49,135 --> 00:55:53,457
But there are some cases where your names are based on local things,

971
00:55:54,245 --> 00:55:56,085
so the names are different in different parts of the world.

972
00:55:56,426 --> 00:55:56,666
Yeah.

973
00:55:56,806 --> 00:56:01,328
The most famous example of this is toward the end of the Paleozoic Era, right,

974
00:56:01,868 --> 00:56:04,769
at a time when forests were dominating the world.

975
00:56:05,069 --> 00:56:06,990
That's a time period that's called the Carboniferous,

976
00:56:07,570 --> 00:56:11,632
because of all the coal that's found there from all the forests and swamps that were around at the time.

977
00:56:11,972 --> 00:56:17,795
But in the United States, it's split into the Mississippian and the Pennsylvanian,

978
00:56:18,125 --> 00:56:23,768
because we have so much information on this time period in this country, on this continent,

979
00:56:24,128 --> 00:56:27,529
that we are able to divide it at a better resolution.

980
00:56:27,890 --> 00:56:28,130
Yes.

981
00:56:28,570 --> 00:56:32,191
Another example is when Will and I were in graduate school,

982
00:56:32,592 --> 00:56:38,534
we learned through the Cenozoic Era, the Age of Mammals, about the North American Land Mammal Ages.

983
00:56:38,954 --> 00:56:39,215
Yep.

984
00:56:39,485 --> 00:56:45,408
Which are time periods, right, we've divided the age of mammals up based not on,

985
00:56:45,448 --> 00:56:50,090
you know, it's the same time period that is split by other means.

986
00:56:50,691 --> 00:56:56,654
Some scientists have attempted to split it specifically based on which mammals are found at which times.

987
00:56:56,954 --> 00:57:00,816
When a certain animal enters the fossil record, and when a certain animal leaves.

988
00:57:01,236 --> 00:57:01,576
Yep.

989
00:57:01,924 --> 00:57:04,905
The trouble is that it only works on one continent.

990
00:57:05,005 --> 00:57:06,266
Yes, and there's a bunch of them.

991
00:57:06,946 --> 00:57:12,509
Yes, exactly, right, because, you know, you have a land mammal age that's based on the appearance of bison.

992
00:57:12,629 --> 00:57:12,789
Yeah.

993
00:57:13,009 --> 00:57:15,250
That's great, but bison came over from Asia.

994
00:57:15,530 --> 00:57:16,811
And there's no Australian bison.

995
00:57:16,891 --> 00:57:21,093
Yes, so it only works in North America, and so you get, you know, you also have

996
00:57:21,584 --> 00:57:28,088
South American Land Mammal Ages, and we try to find as many ways of breaking things up as we possibly can.

997
00:57:28,108 --> 00:57:32,430
Yeah, because as you're saying, if you're going to look at something closely, you know,

998
00:57:32,710 --> 00:57:36,852
if I'm talking about how big an ant is, and I'm using foot as my measurement,

999
00:57:37,112 --> 00:57:40,714
and I'm going to be talking in decimals the whole time, and that's not very clear.

1000
00:57:40,894 --> 00:57:41,154
Yes.

1001
00:57:41,403 --> 00:57:47,926
I break it down into millimeters and, you know, smaller and smaller, you know, microns

1002
00:57:48,346 --> 00:57:49,667
when I'm talking about their body parts.

1003
00:57:49,907 --> 00:57:55,169
Now we can be very specific, and so the more you break it up, the more precision you have.

1004
00:57:55,450 --> 00:57:55,790
Indeed.

1005
00:57:56,350 --> 00:58:00,872
The other thing that's important to remember is that all of these names and all of these

1006
00:58:01,292 --> 00:58:04,454
time periods don't mean anything without context.

1007
00:58:04,674 --> 00:58:04,874
Yes.

1008
00:58:05,223 --> 00:58:11,087
And that's why, you know, that's why commonly we refer to the Cenozoic era as the Age of Mammals,

1009
00:58:11,827 --> 00:58:15,649
because that tells you where it was after the Age of Reptiles.

1010
00:58:16,090 --> 00:58:21,353
The Ice Age, you know, I've talked to a lot of people who have this idea in their head that,

1011
00:58:21,473 --> 00:58:25,075
okay, you had the age of dinosaurs, dinosaurs went extinct, Ice Age happened.

1012
00:58:25,095 --> 00:58:25,235
Yeah.

1013
00:58:25,355 --> 00:58:26,395
All right, well, that's not the case.

1014
00:58:26,502 --> 00:58:32,065
There were tons, there's tons of time, tons of environmental change between those things.

1015
00:58:32,265 --> 00:58:32,565
Yeah.

1016
00:58:32,805 --> 00:58:40,129
And so the reason we have this time scale is so that we can put all of this change into context.

1017
00:58:40,509 --> 00:58:46,652
And that's why whenever we mention time periods on the podcast, I always try to say,

1018
00:58:47,032 --> 00:58:48,453
say, all right, we're talking about the Carboniferous.

1019
00:58:48,805 --> 00:58:54,408
This, by the way, is right before the Age of Dinosaurs started, at a time when Pangaea was

1020
00:58:54,968 --> 00:58:59,750
finishing forming, to give you a sense of where we are in the time frame.

1021
00:58:59,770 --> 00:59:01,771
Yeah, in the order of events.

1022
00:59:02,172 --> 00:59:06,414
Yes, because without context, it doesn't matter unless you know when World War II happened,

1023
00:59:07,114 --> 00:59:07,334
right?

1024
00:59:07,674 --> 00:59:10,036
After World War I and before, you know, other wars,

1025
00:59:11,183 --> 00:59:14,265
you need that context to understand what the situation was.

1026
00:59:14,805 --> 00:59:17,326
So we have this timeline, right?

1027
00:59:17,346 --> 00:59:18,667
We have constructed our timeline.

1028
00:59:18,687 --> 00:59:20,448
We've slapped names on all the periods.

1029
00:59:20,468 --> 00:59:22,249
We've slapped boundaries in between them.

1030
00:59:22,649 --> 00:59:24,770
This is our geologic history.

1031
00:59:25,090 --> 00:59:26,951
The next question is how do we use this?

1032
00:59:27,551 --> 00:59:30,953
Well, not only does it tell us the order of events, not only does it help us say, all right,

1033
00:59:30,973 --> 00:59:35,535
well, you know, the sea level changed and did this and then the fossils did this and this and this.

1034
00:59:36,065 --> 00:59:42,048
It also means we can now put new discoveries in context with the rest of it.

1035
00:59:42,148 --> 00:59:42,408
Yes.

1036
00:59:42,848 --> 00:59:49,351
So we come to the big question, when we find a new fossil or a new fossil layer or a new rock layer,

1037
00:59:49,872 --> 00:59:54,814
how do we know where it goes in the big timeline picture?

1038
00:59:55,154 --> 00:59:57,295
How do we actually give it a placement?

1039
00:59:57,595 --> 00:59:57,996
Indeed.

1040
00:59:58,456 --> 00:59:59,656
There are two answers to this.

1041
00:59:59,922 --> 01:00:04,745
The first answer, the much more common answer, is a technique called relative dating.

1042
01:00:04,845 --> 01:00:05,345
Yeah.

1043
01:00:05,385 --> 01:00:10,088
This is the part where just about every geology professor in the world will make some sort of joke

1044
01:00:10,128 --> 01:00:15,711
about Alabama or their state in the south of choice.

1045
01:00:16,071 --> 01:00:16,231
Yep.

1046
01:00:16,771 --> 01:00:18,392
Relative dating, ha ha ha.

1047
01:00:18,913 --> 01:00:21,174
We at the Common Descent podcast are above that.

1048
01:00:21,394 --> 01:00:21,574
Yes.

1049
01:00:21,954 --> 01:00:24,015
Relative dating, the geologic type.

1050
01:00:24,343 --> 01:00:28,786
Basically what relative dating is saying, it's using the same basic principles we use

1051
01:00:28,806 --> 01:00:30,587
to construct the timeline in the first place.

1052
01:00:31,107 --> 01:00:35,470
You're saying, all right, we understand the sequence of events.

1053
01:00:36,130 --> 01:00:40,393
We have this great image of the overall sequence of events throughout the history of the planet.

1054
01:00:40,853 --> 01:00:44,575
We want to find out our new fossil, our new rock layer.

1055
01:00:45,025 --> 01:00:49,187
Is this before, during, or after other events?

1056
01:00:49,767 --> 01:00:51,047
That's why it's relative dating.

1057
01:00:51,067 --> 01:00:54,669
You're picking a time relative to other things.

1058
01:00:55,249 --> 01:00:57,790
The stratigraphy can of course help, like we said before.

1059
01:00:57,830 --> 01:01:00,411
If you find the KPG boundary, you know where you are.

1060
01:01:00,711 --> 01:01:00,892
Yes.

1061
01:01:00,932 --> 01:01:02,612
You are right at the end of the Cretaceous.

1062
01:01:03,333 --> 01:01:08,035
The biggest method of relative dating is based on fossils.

1063
01:01:08,275 --> 01:01:08,495
Yes.

1064
01:01:08,804 --> 01:01:13,766
Because fossils change the most predictably and the most consistently.

1065
01:01:14,287 --> 01:01:18,849
All of this change, real quick, we should note that the big picture,

1066
01:01:18,869 --> 01:01:22,931
because you see in the news and we talk about on the podcast how there's all these details

1067
01:01:22,951 --> 01:01:24,472
that we're constantly arguing over.

1068
01:01:24,572 --> 01:01:29,914
Oh, did this happen first and the earliest Sicilians are actually back here and they

1069
01:01:29,954 --> 01:01:32,936
weren't over here and this hippo's over here and this and this.

1070
01:01:33,145 --> 01:01:37,087
We mentioned something in the news section about something being older than we thought

1071
01:01:37,127 --> 01:01:39,248
it was almost every episode.

1072
01:01:39,268 --> 01:01:40,749
There's something in the news that mentions,

1073
01:01:40,869 --> 01:01:43,570
oh, it's actually a lot older than we previously thought.

1074
01:01:43,810 --> 01:01:44,050
Yep.

1075
01:01:44,370 --> 01:01:45,491
And that's a very valid point.

1076
01:01:45,771 --> 01:01:50,353
The fine details we are constantly refining and constantly discussing.

1077
01:01:50,673 --> 01:01:54,535
The overall picture has been confirmed and

1078
01:01:54,975 --> 01:01:57,896
refound and corroborated over and over and over and over again.

1079
01:01:57,905 --> 01:02:03,507
We have a very, very good sense of the order of events on the broad scale in Earth history.

1080
01:02:03,527 --> 01:02:03,647
Yeah.

1081
01:02:03,867 --> 01:02:09,230
It's when you hear about those things, quite literally that is a scientist splitting hairs.

1082
01:02:09,650 --> 01:02:10,291
Yeah.

1083
01:02:10,491 --> 01:02:11,671
For good reason, right?

1084
01:02:11,691 --> 01:02:13,732
We're trying to be as specific as possible.

1085
01:02:13,752 --> 01:02:13,912
Yeah.

1086
01:02:14,052 --> 01:02:19,115
It's legitimate stuff, but we are at those points when we're adjusting something by

1087
01:02:19,464 --> 01:02:23,647
just a bit or pushing back when we thought something arrived.

1088
01:02:24,147 --> 01:02:27,449
That is us just tweaking the resolution.

1089
01:02:27,669 --> 01:02:28,329
Not actually.

1090
01:02:28,429 --> 01:02:32,231
It's very rare that, you know, it's never happened since either of us have been alive

1091
01:02:32,251 --> 01:02:34,032
where they've had to go on and go, you know what?

1092
01:02:34,092 --> 01:02:38,575
I think this whole epoch needs to actually be completely shifted.

1093
01:02:38,635 --> 01:02:42,697
It turns out that rabbits actually showed up in the Cambrian period.

1094
01:02:42,757 --> 01:02:42,937
Yes.

1095
01:02:42,957 --> 01:02:43,618
Did not know that.

1096
01:02:43,978 --> 01:02:44,138
Yeah.

1097
01:02:44,142 --> 01:02:44,622
Very strange.

1098
01:02:44,862 --> 01:02:45,723
I don't know what they were eating.

1099
01:02:47,424 --> 01:02:53,007
So fossils are excellent to use for relative placement on the time scale.

1100
01:02:53,287 --> 01:02:57,329
This is called biostratigraphy, the sequence of events in the fossil record.

1101
01:02:57,710 --> 01:03:01,492
If you find dinosaurs, you know you are in the Mesozoic era.

1102
01:03:01,852 --> 01:03:04,473
If you find trilobites, you know you are in the Paleozoic era.

1103
01:03:04,794 --> 01:03:07,555
And this can get very, very specific, right?

1104
01:03:07,865 --> 01:03:13,228
If you have T. rex, for example, T. rex only lived at the very end of the Cretaceous.

1105
01:03:13,388 --> 01:03:13,648
Yes.

1106
01:03:14,088 --> 01:03:20,532
So you have a very good sense of where you are based on when you know these fossils were around.

1107
01:03:20,692 --> 01:03:20,872
Yeah.

1108
01:03:21,172 --> 01:03:26,415
Now this can get a little tricky because there is a trap that you can easily fall into here.

1109
01:03:26,715 --> 01:03:30,957
And to demonstrate this trap, my assistant Will, my lovely assistant.

1110
01:03:31,198 --> 01:03:31,858
Hello.

1111
01:03:31,898 --> 01:03:32,418
Will help me out.

1112
01:03:32,642 --> 01:03:33,163
Play along.

1113
01:03:33,323 --> 01:03:33,543
All right.

1114
01:03:33,783 --> 01:03:36,084
Hey, Will, that's a cool T. rex fossil you have there.

1115
01:03:36,424 --> 01:03:37,185
It sure is.

1116
01:03:37,645 --> 01:03:39,366
What part of the timeline is it from?

1117
01:03:39,506 --> 01:03:41,387
Well, this is from the later Cretaceous.

1118
01:03:41,668 --> 01:03:45,330
Wow, how do you know that that rock layer is from the later Cretaceous?

1119
01:03:45,710 --> 01:03:50,373
Oh, we looked at a bunch of things to see what animals we found there.

1120
01:03:50,673 --> 01:03:54,775
And we know T. rex is from the late Cretaceous, and we found it there.

1121
01:03:54,986 --> 01:03:57,447
Wow, so you have T. rex in your frock layer,

1122
01:03:57,847 --> 01:04:00,388
and you know T. rex is only ever found in the late Cretaceous.

1123
01:04:00,648 --> 01:04:00,889
Yeah.

1124
01:04:01,129 --> 01:04:04,470
How do you know those other rock layers with T. rex in them are from the late Cretaceous?

1125
01:04:04,850 --> 01:04:07,332
Because the T. rex is from the late Cretaceous.

1126
01:04:08,752 --> 01:04:09,233
Wait a minute.

1127
01:04:12,214 --> 01:04:16,616
It can be very easy to fall into this trap of, well, T. rex was in the late Cretaceous,

1128
01:04:16,784 --> 01:04:20,386
so these rocks are late Cretaceous, because all late Cretaceous rocks have T. rex in them,

1129
01:04:20,606 --> 01:04:22,107
because T. rex is from the late Cretaceous,

1130
01:04:22,467 --> 01:04:27,909
and now you have fallen into the dreaded specter of circular reasoning.

1131
01:04:28,409 --> 01:04:31,431
This is a big problem when it comes to dating.

1132
01:04:31,631 --> 01:04:36,433
Because you can have a lot of seps in a circular reasoning argument,

1133
01:04:36,673 --> 01:04:41,595
and it all seems to support itself until you step back and look at it all.

1134
01:04:42,666 --> 01:04:46,768
And that's why we don't use T. rex for biostratigraphy very commonly.

1135
01:04:47,408 --> 01:04:51,930
Typically, when you really want to get an age down, when you really want to figure out where

1136
01:04:51,970 --> 01:04:58,693
you are, we use fossils that are extremely abundant, extremely common, extremely distinctive,

1137
01:04:59,313 --> 01:05:00,874
and we're only around for a brief time.

1138
01:05:01,054 --> 01:05:01,274
Yes.

1139
01:05:01,734 --> 01:05:03,115
We call these index fossils.

1140
01:05:03,695 --> 01:05:06,737
Species of trilobites make great index fossils.

1141
01:05:07,344 --> 01:05:10,145
Species of ammonites make great index fossils.

1142
01:05:10,225 --> 01:05:11,966
We mentioned that in the Cambrian episode.

1143
01:05:12,466 --> 01:05:16,328
Index fossils are really good, because unlike T. rex, who, you know,

1144
01:05:16,348 --> 01:05:22,651
there's 20 good specimens of it, a species of trilobite that you found a thousand of them,

1145
01:05:23,112 --> 01:05:25,313
and they're always before this and after this.

1146
01:05:25,853 --> 01:05:26,053
Yeah.

1147
01:05:26,253 --> 01:05:30,455
So if you want to know, if you're in the late Cretaceous, you don't go with T. rex,

1148
01:05:30,483 --> 01:05:36,486
you say, all right, we have this ammonite species, this type of pollen, and this really abundant,

1149
01:05:36,746 --> 01:05:38,267
and this really abundant mammal.

1150
01:05:39,187 --> 01:05:44,090
Those three, we found a thousand of them, and they're always in this time frame,

1151
01:05:44,110 --> 01:05:46,171
they're always in this time frame, they're always in this time frame.

1152
01:05:46,611 --> 01:05:48,472
That's your biostratigraphy at work.

1153
01:05:48,492 --> 01:05:53,634
Your species are changing, species that change rapidly and achieve abundance and are common

1154
01:05:53,664 --> 01:05:57,006
fossils, great for telling you where in the record you are.

1155
01:05:57,506 --> 01:06:04,109
And overlap is a big part of using that, is you not only want to look for those precise

1156
01:06:04,669 --> 01:06:10,532
index fossils, but if you have a, you know, ammonite that was here for, you know, this,

1157
01:06:11,032 --> 01:06:16,915
you know, these layers or this portion of the Cretaceous, and then a mammal that was there,

1158
01:06:17,306 --> 01:06:21,468
that overlaps with the end of when that ammonite showed up and then continues on,

1159
01:06:21,888 --> 01:06:26,010
those overlaps are really key, because now you can really zero.

1160
01:06:26,030 --> 01:06:30,111
And if you have both, you know that it was, yeah, that's how we, that was one of the ways

1161
01:06:30,151 --> 01:06:35,113
that the fossil site we worked at was dated, was animals that had overlap time frames.

1162
01:06:35,367 --> 01:06:41,631
The only portion of our vast timeline where these species all lived at the same time.

1163
01:06:41,651 --> 01:06:48,216
Another common form of relative dating that can support that is magnetostratigraphy.

1164
01:06:48,369 --> 01:06:48,809
Yeah.

1165
01:06:49,109 --> 01:06:52,651
So one of the weird things that the Earth does is that we have this magnetic field,

1166
01:06:53,231 --> 01:06:59,154
and as time goes on, the magnetic pole moves, and every now and then they flip.

1167
01:06:59,414 --> 01:06:59,574
Yep.

1168
01:07:00,174 --> 01:07:03,655
This does not mean the continents move. I've seen a lot of people get confused about that.

1169
01:07:03,963 --> 01:07:06,965
The continents don't move, it just means that your compass points the other direction.

1170
01:07:07,025 --> 01:07:12,027
That's what causes mass extinction. The Earth flips over, half of the animals fall off.

1171
01:07:13,188 --> 01:07:16,869
All the continents go, and the animals fly off into space.

1172
01:07:18,970 --> 01:07:19,871
No, that's not what happens.

1173
01:07:20,311 --> 01:07:23,173
All it is is the magnetic field, right? North pole becomes south pole,

1174
01:07:23,193 --> 01:07:25,374
south pole becomes north pole, for your compass.

1175
01:07:25,634 --> 01:07:26,954
Yeah, your compass would become confused.

1176
01:07:27,143 --> 01:07:30,305
But when, especially volcanic rocks,

1177
01:07:30,825 --> 01:07:36,408
form and cool, any magnetic minerals in them will line up with the poles.

1178
01:07:36,848 --> 01:07:37,008
Yep.

1179
01:07:37,028 --> 01:07:44,312
So you can tell by looking at your rocks, was this a time period of normal polarity,

1180
01:07:44,372 --> 01:07:47,114
like it is today, or reversed polarity compared to today.

1181
01:07:47,566 --> 01:07:51,828
And then if you have those other species, right, you say, all right, well,

1182
01:07:52,268 --> 01:07:57,131
every time we've ever found this ammonite species, not only was it in the Late Cretaceous,

1183
01:07:57,471 --> 01:08:04,234
it also corresponds to a reversed polarity, so we can double check the rocks and say, all right,

1184
01:08:04,254 --> 01:08:09,356
yeah, we are in a period of reversed polarity. So you can see we have this timeline of the

1185
01:08:09,406 --> 01:08:14,748
magnetic shifts, and that can help refine your time even further.

1186
01:08:14,828 --> 01:08:15,048
Yeah.

1187
01:08:15,368 --> 01:08:20,150
This, once again, gets back to that word that I loved from before, corroboration.

1188
01:08:21,251 --> 01:08:27,213
It is rare that we will use one index fossil, or it is rare that we will use just magnetostratigraphy.

1189
01:08:27,253 --> 01:08:33,015
We are going to use as many of these as possible to get as confident as we can be on this age.

1190
01:08:33,906 --> 01:08:39,769
That's a big part of most studies, is you want to find your answer makes sense

1191
01:08:40,509 --> 01:08:42,090
from more than one point of view.

1192
01:08:42,490 --> 01:08:42,750
Yes.

1193
01:08:42,990 --> 01:08:44,791
Measure it with as many tools as you have.

1194
01:08:45,231 --> 01:08:49,033
And that's exactly what this is, is biostratigraphy and magnetostratigraphy are

1195
01:08:49,053 --> 01:08:55,175
both two different tools for trying to establish when a fossil was formed.

1196
01:08:55,215 --> 01:08:55,595
Indeed.

1197
01:08:55,964 --> 01:08:59,206
And there are a bunch of other things that you can do of a similar vein.

1198
01:08:59,246 --> 01:09:05,108
If you're very recent, you can use the sequence of tools that you're finding in human sites.

1199
01:09:05,949 --> 01:09:06,949
You can use pollen, right?

1200
01:09:06,969 --> 01:09:12,192
Pollen is a great thing to show you're changing, sometimes changing of species,

1201
01:09:12,312 --> 01:09:15,793
but also changing of climatic regimes, right?

1202
01:09:15,913 --> 01:09:17,194
Changing of...

1203
01:09:17,544 --> 01:09:23,547
You know, you can look at sea level changes over time to see this species existed at this,

1204
01:09:23,967 --> 01:09:28,829
at a period of increased sea level, which corresponds with a magnetic signature of this,

1205
01:09:28,869 --> 01:09:30,130
and you can line them all up.

1206
01:09:30,430 --> 01:09:35,012
One of the really interesting things that I learned recently is that if you are looking

1207
01:09:35,753 --> 01:09:41,756
recently enough, your magnetic minerals in your rocks not only flip with the poles,

1208
01:09:42,126 --> 01:09:45,367
but will trace the migration of the North Pole.

1209
01:09:46,267 --> 01:09:48,868
Because the North Pole doesn't stay still, it's always moving around.

1210
01:09:49,528 --> 01:09:55,951
And for the last short, you know, in very recent times, you know, recent-ish human history,

1211
01:09:56,411 --> 01:10:01,533
we understand this migration at a high enough resolution that you can look at rocks and say,

1212
01:10:01,553 --> 01:10:07,796
all right, the pole was over here, which tells us where we are in history, which is pretty cool.

1213
01:10:08,046 --> 01:10:10,607
So relative dating is really, like I said before,

1214
01:10:10,928 --> 01:10:16,810
we are identifying are we before, during, or after something else, right?

1215
01:10:16,890 --> 01:10:21,352
This rock layer happened during this magnetic anomaly.

1216
01:10:21,612 --> 01:10:26,775
This happened before the extinction of this species, but after the appearance of this species.

1217
01:10:27,175 --> 01:10:28,636
That is your relative dating.

1218
01:10:28,676 --> 01:10:32,157
And you can place it, all right, here we are, anywhere in your timeline.

1219
01:10:32,524 --> 01:10:34,244
I have this nifty new fossil.

1220
01:10:34,625 --> 01:10:36,045
It has all these things.

1221
01:10:36,465 --> 01:10:41,368
The only place in the timeline that fits all that information is right there in the Late Cretaceous.

1222
01:10:41,468 --> 01:10:46,030
Yeah, and you're putting things in between other events or among.

1223
01:10:46,450 --> 01:10:48,171
It's all about putting things in order.

1224
01:10:48,291 --> 01:10:48,571
Yes.

1225
01:10:49,312 --> 01:10:55,835
The other way that you can infer where your new discovery goes on the time scale

1226
01:10:56,265 --> 01:10:58,066
is what's called absolute dating.

1227
01:10:58,406 --> 01:11:04,489
And absolute dating is putting actual numbers on your fossil or on your rock layer.

1228
01:11:05,029 --> 01:11:07,170
It is worth noting, and we did this on purpose,

1229
01:11:07,530 --> 01:11:11,732
that Will and I have gone this entire discussion of the geologic time scale

1230
01:11:11,792 --> 01:11:14,674
and Earth history without mentioning any numbers.

1231
01:11:14,834 --> 01:11:15,134
Yes.

1232
01:11:15,663 --> 01:11:21,806
And the reason we did that on purpose is because you don't need numbers to have this time scale.

1233
01:11:22,086 --> 01:11:25,288
You don't need numbers to know the orders of events.

1234
01:11:25,668 --> 01:11:30,070
And indeed, much of the history of geology took place

1235
01:11:30,150 --> 01:11:33,992
without us knowing the numbers that went along with these fossils and these layers.

1236
01:11:34,452 --> 01:11:39,735
Geology kicked off, you know, really kicked off in the 1600s to the 1700s.

1237
01:11:40,185 --> 01:11:43,126
We didn't really start getting a good handle on

1238
01:11:43,627 --> 01:11:48,289
getting actual numbers on fossils and rock layers until the early 1900s.

1239
01:11:48,929 --> 01:11:53,091
But nowadays, it's extremely common, and we do this constantly.

1240
01:11:53,111 --> 01:11:56,792
And nowadays, we have actually been able to put numbers on the time scale,

1241
01:11:56,812 --> 01:11:59,653
which is really, really cool when you think about it.

1242
01:11:59,753 --> 01:12:04,636
And this is the dating that you typically hear about, is where we're adjusting,

1243
01:12:04,665 --> 01:12:09,367
you know, exactly how many million years do we need to shift something,

1244
01:12:09,407 --> 01:12:13,169
instead of, you know, how, what place does this fall?

1245
01:12:13,349 --> 01:12:16,130
Right. Was it the late Cretaceous? Was it the late, late Cretaceous?

1246
01:12:16,170 --> 01:12:17,431
Was it the early, late Cretaceous?

1247
01:12:17,631 --> 01:12:17,811
Yeah.

1248
01:12:17,911 --> 01:12:19,292
This is us saying numbers.

1249
01:12:19,612 --> 01:12:24,234
The most common and most famous form of absolute dating is radiometric dating.

1250
01:12:24,494 --> 01:12:24,655
Yes.

1251
01:12:25,004 --> 01:12:32,607
Radiometric dating is based on this principle that certain elements have forms, or isotopes,

1252
01:12:32,787 --> 01:12:34,008
that break down over time.

1253
01:12:34,728 --> 01:12:40,151
If you have potassium-40, give it time, and potassium-40 will gradually break down into

1254
01:12:40,271 --> 01:12:40,871
argon-40.

1255
01:12:41,211 --> 01:12:44,253
Uranium-238 will gradually break down into lead.

1256
01:12:44,553 --> 01:12:47,714
Carbon-14 will gradually break down into nitrogen-14.

1257
01:12:48,185 --> 01:12:54,608
We can study these materials and understand the rate of this breakdown,

1258
01:12:54,648 --> 01:12:58,049
the rate of their decay, and then you find a rock and you say,

1259
01:12:58,069 --> 01:13:00,711
"All right, well, this is how much decay has happened.

1260
01:13:00,731 --> 01:13:02,832
This is how much those chemicals have changed.

1261
01:13:03,212 --> 01:13:05,633
We know the rate that that change happens,

1262
01:13:05,993 --> 01:13:09,735
which means we know how long it takes for this much decay to happen."

1263
01:13:10,095 --> 01:13:11,056
It's really as simple as that.

1264
01:13:11,216 --> 01:13:12,337
Yeah.

1265
01:13:12,477 --> 01:13:13,817
Right?

1266
01:13:13,943 --> 01:13:15,604
This is how much decay has happened.

1267
01:13:15,984 --> 01:13:17,325
We've studied the rate.

1268
01:13:17,425 --> 01:13:20,166
It takes 3 million years for that to happen.

1269
01:13:20,847 --> 01:13:21,667
And now you have your number.

1270
01:13:21,927 --> 01:13:25,790
The most common, the most famous form of radiometric dating is carbon dating.

1271
01:13:26,190 --> 01:13:27,331
Everybody knows carbon dating.

1272
01:13:27,691 --> 01:13:29,652
Carbon dating is based on the same principle.

1273
01:13:29,692 --> 01:13:34,094
Carbon dating is very unique in that it can be used on organic material.

1274
01:13:34,615 --> 01:13:36,856
You can date fossils.

1275
01:13:36,904 --> 01:13:40,146
You can date man-made items in some cases.

1276
01:13:40,246 --> 01:13:41,326
You can date wood.

1277
01:13:41,766 --> 01:13:42,067
Yeah.

1278
01:13:42,367 --> 01:13:46,809
I mean, they've dated tortoise shell from even living tortoises before.

1279
01:13:47,329 --> 01:13:47,609
Yup.

1280
01:13:47,989 --> 01:13:52,011
So carbon dating is really cool because you can actually directly date your fossils because they

1281
01:13:52,031 --> 01:13:55,273
have carbon in them, but carbon dating is also very limited.

1282
01:13:55,613 --> 01:14:00,655
Carbon dating only really works back to about 50,000, 60,000 years.

1283
01:14:01,505 --> 01:14:04,627
The Earth is about four and a half billion years old,

1284
01:14:05,587 --> 01:14:09,129
which means carbon dating is extraordinarily useful

1285
01:14:09,609 --> 01:14:12,671
in a very, very, very, very, very slim sliver of time.

1286
01:14:13,011 --> 01:14:15,312
It's super popular for archaeologists.

1287
01:14:15,772 --> 01:14:19,194
Absolutely, and if you study late ice age, it's very popular.

1288
01:14:19,574 --> 01:14:23,596
For the rest of the time scale, the nice thing is that carbon dating is not the only radiometric

1289
01:14:23,616 --> 01:14:23,936
dating.

1290
01:14:24,244 --> 01:14:27,966
You can use potassium argon, which is helpful when you're...

1291
01:14:28,046 --> 01:14:30,267
You can find it in volcanic rocks most of the time.

1292
01:14:30,767 --> 01:14:36,571
Uranium is a great element to study because it's fairly common in certain minerals,

1293
01:14:37,051 --> 01:14:42,254
and uranium actually has two different isotopes that decay at two different rates.

1294
01:14:43,294 --> 01:14:46,616
So if you find a sample of uranium, you can actually get two dates from it.

1295
01:14:46,816 --> 01:14:47,036
Yeah.

1296
01:14:47,343 --> 01:14:49,764
Which is really nice because you get that corroboration once again.

1297
01:14:49,784 --> 01:14:51,045
You're checking yourself.

1298
01:14:51,545 --> 01:14:55,267
Uranium decays into lead, into thorium, into protactinium.

1299
01:14:55,287 --> 01:14:57,188
You can use samarium neodymium dating.

1300
01:14:57,208 --> 01:14:58,949
You can use rubidium strontium dating.

1301
01:14:59,189 --> 01:15:04,011
There is a long, long list of elements that decay at a certain rate,

1302
01:15:04,051 --> 01:15:07,613
that we understand that rate, that are found in certain materials,

1303
01:15:07,973 --> 01:15:11,495
that you can examine them, figure out how much this changed,

1304
01:15:11,885 --> 01:15:13,766
calculate the age based on that.

1305
01:15:13,926 --> 01:15:20,529
And because they each have differing rates, each one can cover a different age of the earth.

1306
01:15:21,009 --> 01:15:21,309
Yes.

1307
01:15:21,450 --> 01:15:25,932
Different ones will apply to older or younger, and some of them may overlap,

1308
01:15:26,192 --> 01:15:28,513
and you can get differing...

1309
01:15:28,613 --> 01:15:31,675
You can use different ones to date whatever it is you're looking at,

1310
01:15:31,715 --> 01:15:32,715
depending on what they have.

1311
01:15:32,995 --> 01:15:33,315
Indeed.

1312
01:15:33,823 --> 01:15:37,645
Carbon dating is good, like I said, back to maybe 50,000 years,

1313
01:15:37,945 --> 01:15:40,907
but potassium dating goes back billions of years.

1314
01:15:41,607 --> 01:15:44,189
Uranium dating goes back effectively forever.

1315
01:15:44,729 --> 01:15:47,391
It's got such a long rate of decay,

1316
01:15:47,731 --> 01:15:51,673
which makes it sometimes not quite as good for really young things.

1317
01:15:51,753 --> 01:15:52,033
Yes.

1318
01:15:52,414 --> 01:15:54,315
But excellent for very, very old things.

1319
01:15:54,855 --> 01:15:56,276
Because eventually you reach a point where

1320
01:15:56,783 --> 01:15:59,084
so much has decayed that you can't really measure it anymore.

1321
01:15:59,224 --> 01:16:01,045
Yeah, you lose that resolution again.

1322
01:16:01,365 --> 01:16:03,766
Or so little has decayed that you can't really tell.

1323
01:16:03,946 --> 01:16:05,147
You have to get it in that sweet spot.

1324
01:16:05,447 --> 01:16:07,328
Radiometric dates come with errors, right?

1325
01:16:07,348 --> 01:16:11,610
It's not telling you to the day, so usually you'll see...

1326
01:16:11,670 --> 01:16:16,512
It'll say, we dated this to 98 million years, plus or minus 0.6 million.

1327
01:16:16,552 --> 01:16:18,513
Yeah, it doesn't have a shelf life date on it.

1328
01:16:18,693 --> 01:16:18,933
Yes.

1329
01:16:19,313 --> 01:16:20,014
Best used by...

1330
01:16:20,064 --> 01:16:20,484
It doesn't have.

1331
01:16:21,745 --> 01:16:27,652
Yeah, so now that is a date with a resolution of a little over a million years,

1332
01:16:28,466 --> 01:16:31,828
which sounds like a lot, but is actually...

1333
01:16:32,248 --> 01:16:33,889
That's like a 6%...

1334
01:16:34,109 --> 01:16:38,692
No, that's less. That's 0.6%, which is pretty good.

1335
01:16:38,852 --> 01:16:39,992
That's pretty high resolution.

1336
01:16:40,032 --> 01:16:43,334
A lot of our radiometric dates are really, really solid these days.

1337
01:16:43,864 --> 01:16:48,126
There are other forms of direct dating, of absolute dating.

1338
01:16:48,686 --> 01:16:51,568
Some are also based on breakdown over time.

1339
01:16:52,028 --> 01:16:56,970
Fission track dating is basically when uranium decays,

1340
01:16:57,030 --> 01:17:00,332
it leaves tracks through minerals, which you can count.

1341
01:17:00,768 --> 01:17:06,952
Thermoluminescence is a dating that measures the disruption in electrons

1342
01:17:06,972 --> 01:17:09,474
by radiation of minerals over time.

1343
01:17:09,867 --> 01:17:14,369
So the longer they're being exposed to radiation, like the sun or something,

1344
01:17:14,689 --> 01:17:16,570
the more you're getting this change.

1345
01:17:17,230 --> 01:17:18,551
There are a few things that look at that.

1346
01:17:18,871 --> 01:17:23,853
There's a handful that look at how do atoms and chemicals change over time.

1347
01:17:24,213 --> 01:17:29,255
Things that change in consistent rates, we can use them to calculate an age.

1348
01:17:30,346 --> 01:17:34,388
Another really interesting version of this that is for living things,

1349
01:17:34,448 --> 01:17:37,289
or once living things, is looking at amino acids.

1350
01:17:37,989 --> 01:17:41,271
If you have a fossil that's only a few ten thousand years old,

1351
01:17:41,311 --> 01:17:46,593
or hundreds of thousands of years old, amino acids will change their form.

1352
01:17:46,613 --> 01:17:48,794
So these are the molecules that make up DNA.

1353
01:17:49,494 --> 01:17:52,856
They will change their form gradually over time.

1354
01:17:53,204 --> 01:17:56,225
That amino acids tend to be left-handed and right-handed,

1355
01:17:56,585 --> 01:18:00,067
but as time goes on, the left-handed ones change into right-handed form

1356
01:18:00,567 --> 01:18:03,228
once the living organism stops repairing things.

1357
01:18:03,328 --> 01:18:03,568
Yeah.

1358
01:18:03,829 --> 01:18:07,070
So that happens at a consistent rate, and you can look at it and say,

1359
01:18:07,110 --> 01:18:10,371
"Oh, okay, this has been around for this amount of time."

1360
01:18:10,552 --> 01:18:11,092
Yeah.

1361
01:18:11,192 --> 01:18:13,733
In some of these cases, you might not get an exact amount of time,

1362
01:18:13,753 --> 01:18:16,554
but it might be, "Okay, this has been around for at least this long,

1363
01:18:16,624 --> 01:18:20,946
or at most this long, or somewhere between these time periods."

1364
01:18:21,666 --> 01:18:27,969
Once again, with that corroboration, you can, using that range, if you use other techniques as well,

1365
01:18:28,709 --> 01:18:32,031
where they overlap, can give you even more precision.

1366
01:18:32,611 --> 01:18:36,433
As we said earlier, never just using the single technique and being like,

1367
01:18:36,453 --> 01:18:38,574
"All right, done. Dated. Good to go."

1368
01:18:38,804 --> 01:18:42,546
In fact, that 98 million years plus or minus 0.6 that I said before

1369
01:18:42,726 --> 01:18:48,649
is actually the example from the bird in amber that we talked about last episode.

1370
01:18:49,130 --> 01:18:53,472
It was dated directly using, I think, uranium, and that's the number they got,

1371
01:18:53,872 --> 01:18:58,594
and then it was corroborated with index fossils from the same layers,

1372
01:18:59,305 --> 01:19:04,667
which correspond to time periods that have been dated by other methods

1373
01:19:05,148 --> 01:19:07,349
to between 95 and 105 million years.

1374
01:19:08,169 --> 01:19:10,310
So you have two different methods telling you the same thing,

1375
01:19:10,450 --> 01:19:12,611
which is how science works. That's corroboration.

1376
01:19:13,031 --> 01:19:16,292
A couple other forms of absolute dating that are very famous

1377
01:19:16,472 --> 01:19:19,974
and not based on decay or change or chemicals.

1378
01:19:20,764 --> 01:19:27,467
Tree rings, dendrochronology, right? Trees create new rings on a yearly basis,

1379
01:19:27,828 --> 01:19:32,610
and depending on the year, the water, you know, how much water is available,

1380
01:19:32,630 --> 01:19:36,552
what the temperatures were like, you will see the rings will have slightly different shapes

1381
01:19:36,632 --> 01:19:39,993
and slightly different features, and you can count them,

1382
01:19:40,093 --> 01:19:42,494
one, two, three, four, five, six, all the way back as far as you want,

1383
01:19:42,944 --> 01:19:48,367
and if the ring shape is identifying a particular event, like a drought,

1384
01:19:48,827 --> 01:19:53,350
you can correlate that with older trees and stack up your trees

1385
01:19:53,410 --> 01:19:55,671
the same way we correlated our geologic layers,

1386
01:19:56,211 --> 01:19:59,673
and tree rings can take you back 10,000 years or more.

1387
01:20:00,674 --> 01:20:03,575
And one of the coolest things about that is that tree ring dating,

1388
01:20:03,665 --> 01:20:08,807
or the other one which is basically looking at certain places lay down rock layers

1389
01:20:08,847 --> 01:20:10,908
at a set rate. Yes.

1390
01:20:11,068 --> 01:20:14,830
So there's, for example, there's a lake in Japan whose name I don't remember off the top of my head,

1391
01:20:15,070 --> 01:20:18,872
but every year a new layer of silt is laid down,

1392
01:20:19,452 --> 01:20:25,615
and if you drill down through it, you see these layers going back tens of thousands of years.

1393
01:20:25,765 --> 01:20:29,147
Yeah, so if you have, you know, if you had a yearly flood in an area,

1394
01:20:29,287 --> 01:20:33,909
you have floodplains, you know, to where it brings in a bunch of water

1395
01:20:33,949 --> 01:20:38,571
and lays down silt or lays down nutrients or lays down fish materials,

1396
01:20:39,071 --> 01:20:44,313
and you can tell that's a yearly cycle, you can now just count the years and the layers.

1397
01:20:44,693 --> 01:20:50,275
And the wonderful thing about this is that you can use these different absolute methods

1398
01:20:50,365 --> 01:20:56,148
to check each other. So people, you know, a lot of people get iffy around carbon dating

